1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,368
Welcome to chapter six of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,368 --> 00:00:09,136
This is the survival guide to tensors.

3
00:00:09,136 --> 00:00:16,639
Every number inside a language model lives in a tensor, and almost every bug you'll hit is a shape bug.

4
00:00:16,639 --> 00:00:32,081
So today we learn to think in shapes: creating tensors, reshaping them, slicing, broadcasting, matrix multiplication, attention in four lines, autograd, nn dot Module, embeddings, and the five step training loop.

5
00:00:32,081 --> 00:00:41,332
I ran the book's chapter six notebook on a CPU for this video, and I'll show the real outputs plus a few experiments of my own.

6
00:00:42,182 --> 00:00:45,905
In chapter five we turned text into a list of token IDs.

7
00:00:45,905 --> 00:00:52,887
Today those IDs become tensors, then embeddings, then we run attention on them and write a training loop.

8
00:00:52,887 --> 00:00:57,542
From chapter seven, part three of the book, we build the model itself.

9
00:00:57,542 --> 00:01:02,661
Everything today follows the chapter six notebook, linked in the description.

10
00:01:03,512 --> 00:01:04,621
Here's the plan.

11
00:01:04,621 --> 00:01:08,713
Why tensors, and what changes moving from NumPy to PyTorch.

12
00:01:08,713 --> 00:01:11,835
Then building up dimensions from one to four.

13
00:01:11,835 --> 00:01:15,164
Creating tensors, seeds, data types and devices.

14
00:01:15,164 --> 00:01:18,770
Reshaping, including the view versus reshape puzzle.

15
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:22,377
Indexing, broadcasting and the essential operations.

16
00:01:22,377 --> 00:01:24,597
Attention in four lines of code.

17
00:01:24,597 --> 00:01:27,648
Then autograd, nn dot Module and embeddings.

18
00:01:27,648 --> 00:01:32,504
And finally the five step training loop, the exercises and the review.

19
00:01:33,353 --> 00:01:34,410
The vocabulary.

20
00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:39,127
A tensor is a multi-dimensional array with a shape and a data type.

21
00:01:39,127 --> 00:01:43,774
The shape lists its dimensions, like batch, sequence and features.

22
00:01:43,774 --> 00:01:48,703
Broadcasting virtually expands a smaller tensor to match a bigger one.

23
00:01:48,703 --> 00:01:54,618
The at sign is matrix multiplication, the core of attention and feed forward layers.

24
00:01:54,618 --> 00:02:03,771
Autograd records operations and computes gradients, and a gradient tells you how much the loss changes when you nudge a parameter.

25
00:02:03,771 --> 00:02:09,967
nn dot Module is PyTorch's building block that bundles parameters with a forward method.

26
00:02:09,967 --> 00:02:16,867
The loss measures how wrong we are, and the device is where a tensor lives, on the CPU or the GPU.

27
00:02:17,717 --> 00:02:18,431
Part one.

28
00:02:18,431 --> 00:02:19,381
Why tensors?

29
00:02:20,231 --> 00:02:26,366
Python lists are like loose pages on a desk: fine for scribbles, terrible for maths.

30
00:02:26,366 --> 00:02:31,551
Tensors are organised stacks of numbers that the computer can optimise.

31
00:02:31,551 --> 00:02:34,326
Every tensor is just how many of what.

32
00:02:34,326 --> 00:02:36,006
A scalar is one number.

33
00:02:36,006 --> 00:02:38,124
A vector is a row of numbers.

34
00:02:38,124 --> 00:02:40,169
A matrix is rows by columns.

35
00:02:40,169 --> 00:02:47,764
A three dimensional tensor is a stack of matrices, and a four dimensional tensor is a cabinet of stacks.

36
00:02:47,764 --> 00:03:01,420
In LLM code you'll mostly see three shapes: token IDs as batch by sequence, embeddings as batch by sequence by model width, and attention scores as batch by heads by sequence by sequence.

37
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:06,699
The good news is that PyTorch feels like NumPy with extras.

38
00:03:06,699 --> 00:03:10,003
Creating an array becomes creating a tensor.

39
00:03:10,003 --> 00:03:13,681
Matrix multiplication is the at operator in both.

40
00:03:13,681 --> 00:03:21,789
Changing type uses dot astype in NumPy and dot to in PyTorch, and dot to also moves tensors between devices.

41
00:03:21,789 --> 00:03:28,396
Defaults differ: NumPy floats are float sixty four, PyTorch floats are float thirty two.

42
00:03:28,396 --> 00:03:29,522
I checked both.

43
00:03:29,522 --> 00:03:35,528
For deep learning float thirty two is what you want: half the memory and faster.

44
00:03:35,528 --> 00:03:40,558
And PyTorch adds the two superpowers: GPUs and automatic gradients.

45
00:03:41,408 --> 00:03:43,003
Two practical things.

46
00:03:43,003 --> 00:03:53,560
Devices: you move a tensor to the GPU with dot to cuda, and you can't mix CPU and GPU tensors in one operation, or you get a runtime error.

47
00:03:53,560 --> 00:04:02,295
GPUs are often ten to a hundred times faster for this kind of work, although everything in this video ran on a CPU.

48
00:04:02,295 --> 00:04:11,181
And the zero copy bridge: torch dot from numpy shares memory with the NumPy array, so changing one changes the other.

49
00:04:11,181 --> 00:04:19,156
I ran it: set element zero of the NumPy array to nine nine nine, and the tensor shows nine nine nine too.

50
00:04:19,156 --> 00:04:23,257
If you want an independent copy, use torch dot tensor.

51
00:04:24,108 --> 00:04:24,720
Part two.

52
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:26,284
Building up dimensions.

53
00:04:27,134 --> 00:04:29,194
Let's connect to chapter five.

54
00:04:29,194 --> 00:04:35,102
Our tokenizer gave us two, three, four, six: a one dimensional tensor with shape four.

55
00:04:35,102 --> 00:04:41,765
Add a second sentence and we have a batch, a two by four matrix: two sentences, four tokens each.

56
00:04:41,765 --> 00:04:51,245
Replace every ID with its seven sixty eight dimensional embedding and we get a three dimensional tensor, two by four by seven sixty eight.

57
00:04:51,245 --> 00:04:59,901
The book's filing cabinet analogy fits well: the drawer is the sentence, the folder is the token, and the page is one feature.

58
00:04:59,901 --> 00:05:07,938
And multi head attention adds a fourth dimension: two by eight by four by four, one four by four score grid per head.

59
00:05:08,788 --> 00:05:12,133
Here's the notebook's real output for those steps.

60
00:05:12,133 --> 00:05:23,841
Shape four, then two by four, then two by four by seven sixty eight, where a single token's embedding is seven sixty eight numbers, then the four dimensional attention tensor.

61
00:05:23,841 --> 00:05:33,876
Get used to the names, because they're everywhere in transformer code: B for batch, S or L for sequence length, d model for features, and H for heads.

62
00:05:34,726 --> 00:05:35,455
Part three.

63
00:05:35,455 --> 00:05:36,582
Creating tensors.

64
00:05:37,432 --> 00:05:39,836
There are four ways to fill a tensor.

65
00:05:39,836 --> 00:05:42,499
From existing data with torch dot tensor.

66
00:05:42,499 --> 00:05:50,425
With a fill rule: zeros, ones, uniform random with rand, normally distributed with randn, or random integers with randint.

67
00:05:50,425 --> 00:05:56,726
As evenly spaced number lines, with arange and linspace, which are handy for positions and masks.

68
00:05:56,726 --> 00:06:02,638
And by choosing the kind of number and where it lives, with the dtype and device arguments.

69
00:06:02,638 --> 00:06:06,211
Set those deliberately rather than relying on defaults.

70
00:06:07,061 --> 00:06:09,804
Computers don't make truly random numbers.

71
00:06:09,804 --> 00:06:13,526
They run a formula from a starting point called the seed.

72
00:06:13,526 --> 00:06:21,493
Set the same seed and you get exactly the same random numbers, which is essential for debugging and comparing experiments.

73
00:06:21,493 --> 00:06:24,040
The notebook checks it and prints true.

74
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:24,955
Why forty two?

75
00:06:24,955 --> 00:06:28,285
It's a nod to the Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy.

76
00:06:28,285 --> 00:06:29,461
Any integer works.

77
00:06:30,311 --> 00:06:31,033
Part four.

78
00:06:31,033 --> 00:06:32,551
Shapes and reshaping.

79
00:06:33,401 --> 00:06:37,679
Shapes are contracts between your code and the operations you call.

80
00:06:37,679 --> 00:06:49,557
Try to add a tensor ending in seven sixty eight to one ending in five twelve, and PyTorch refuses with a clear error: size seven sixty eight must match size five twelve at dimension two.

81
00:06:49,557 --> 00:06:57,475
Read shapes from left to right like nested containers: two batches, each of four tokens, each of seven sixty eight features.

82
00:06:57,475 --> 00:06:59,391
And print shapes all the time.

83
00:06:59,391 --> 00:07:02,009
Half of all deep learning bugs live here.

84
00:07:02,859 --> 00:07:04,411
The reshaping toolkit.

85
00:07:04,411 --> 00:07:14,215
reshape rearranges the same numbers into a new shape, and the total must stay the same: twelve can be three by four, but not three by five.

86
00:07:14,215 --> 00:07:17,318
Minus one means infer this dimension for me.

87
00:07:17,318 --> 00:07:24,019
unsqueeze adds a dimension of size one, typically to make a single example into a batch of one.

88
00:07:24,019 --> 00:07:26,558
squeeze removes size one dimensions.

89
00:07:26,558 --> 00:07:31,707
And permute reorders dimensions, for example swapping heads and sequence.

90
00:07:31,707 --> 00:07:39,184
Below is the notebook's real output, walking a tensor through view, reshape, squeeze, permute and flatten.

91
00:07:40,033 --> 00:07:42,096
Why does view sometimes fail?

92
00:07:42,096 --> 00:07:45,155
Think of memory as numbered boxes in a row.

93
00:07:45,155 --> 00:07:53,405
A freshly created tensor is contiguous, stored in order, so view can just relabel the boxes without moving anything.

94
00:07:53,405 --> 00:08:00,803
After a transpose or permute, the logical order jumps around memory: zero, four, eight, one, five, nine.

95
00:08:00,803 --> 00:08:04,075
view refuses to move data and raises an error.

96
00:08:04,075 --> 00:08:08,485
I triggered it on the permuted heads tensor from exercise two.

97
00:08:08,485 --> 00:08:11,970
reshape is happy to make a copy when it needs to.

98
00:08:11,970 --> 00:08:15,526
So the book's advice is right: default to reshape.

99
00:08:16,376 --> 00:08:17,077
Part five.

100
00:08:17,077 --> 00:08:20,792
Indexing, broadcasting, and the essential operations.

101
00:08:21,642 --> 00:08:25,589
Indexing works like Python lists, one dimension at a time.

102
00:08:25,589 --> 00:08:27,563
x zero takes the first batch.

103
00:08:27,563 --> 00:08:34,163
A colon keeps a whole dimension, and colon minus one drops the last element, here the last token.

104
00:08:34,163 --> 00:08:38,314
Three dots, the ellipsis, means all the remaining dimensions.

105
00:08:38,314 --> 00:08:44,439
And torch dot tril, lower triangle, builds the causal mask from chapter three in one line.

106
00:08:44,439 --> 00:08:47,093
The outputs here are from the notebook.

107
00:08:47,943 --> 00:08:52,614
Broadcasting lets you combine tensors of different shapes without copying.

108
00:08:52,614 --> 00:08:55,643
Add a three by four matrix and a vector of four.

109
00:08:55,643 --> 00:09:01,766
PyTorch virtually repeats the vector for each row, the dashed cells, and adds element by element.

110
00:09:01,766 --> 00:09:05,300
Eleven, twenty two, thirty three, forty four, and so on.

111
00:09:05,300 --> 00:09:14,200
The rules, read from the right: two dimensions are compatible if they're equal or if one of them is one, and missing dimensions count as one.

112
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:15,399
Otherwise it fails.

113
00:09:16,249 --> 00:09:18,548
Here's exercise one, which I ran.

114
00:09:18,548 --> 00:09:29,624
A four by three by seven sixty eight tensor plus a bias of seven sixty eight broadcasts across batch and sequence: that's how every linear layer adds its bias.

115
00:09:29,624 --> 00:09:34,640
The exercise asks what happens with a three by seven sixty eight tensor.

116
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:35,546
It works too.

117
00:09:35,546 --> 00:09:42,791
Aligning from the right, three matches three and the batch dimension is missing, so it's treated as one.

118
00:09:42,791 --> 00:09:48,086
But four by seven sixty eight fails, because it lines up four against three.

119
00:09:48,086 --> 00:09:54,216
And the causal mask, shape one by one by S by S, broadcasts across every batch and head.

120
00:09:55,066 --> 00:09:58,840
The operations you'll use ninety percent of the time.

121
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:02,828
Star is element-wise, while at is matrix multiplication.

122
00:10:02,828 --> 00:10:12,155
A thirty two by ten by sixty four tensor times a sixty four by one twenty eight matrix gives thirty two by ten by one twenty eight.

123
00:10:12,155 --> 00:10:16,357
That's how a linear layer mixes features at every position.

124
00:10:16,357 --> 00:10:23,620
Reductions like sum and mean take a dim argument, and keepdim keeps the reduced dimension as size one.

125
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:28,533
cat joins along an existing dimension, while stack creates a new one.

126
00:10:28,533 --> 00:10:32,164
And softmax with dim minus one normalises each row.

127
00:10:32,164 --> 00:10:38,858
The two classic bugs are confusing star with at, and running softmax over the wrong dimension.

128
00:10:39,708 --> 00:10:40,452
Part six.

129
00:10:40,452 --> 00:10:42,438
Attention in four lines.

130
00:10:43,288 --> 00:10:48,191
Here's chapter three's attention as real code, from the notebook.

131
00:10:48,191 --> 00:10:52,415
Q, K and V are random two by five by sixty four tensors.

132
00:10:52,415 --> 00:11:00,109
Step one: Q times K transposed over the last two dimensions gives a five by five score grid per batch.

133
00:11:00,109 --> 00:11:05,088
Step two: divide by the square root of sixty four, which is eight.

134
00:11:05,088 --> 00:11:10,293
Step three: softmax over the last dimension, so each row sums to one.

135
00:11:10,293 --> 00:11:13,687
Step four: multiply by V to blend the values.

136
00:11:13,687 --> 00:11:16,931
The output has the same shape as the input.

137
00:11:16,931 --> 00:11:22,664
In my run, token two put seventy six percent of its attention on token zero.

138
00:11:23,514 --> 00:11:24,934
Add the causal mask.

139
00:11:24,934 --> 00:11:36,013
The notebook builds an upper triangle of trues above the diagonal, the future, and fills those scores with minus infinity, which becomes zero after softmax.

140
00:11:36,013 --> 00:11:41,907
Token two's weights now end in two zeros: it can only see tokens zero, one and two.

141
00:11:41,907 --> 00:11:50,003
In practice, PyTorch does all of this in one call: F dot scaled dot product attention, with is causal equals true.

142
00:11:50,003 --> 00:11:55,117
I checked that my manual version and the one liner give the same result.

143
00:11:55,117 --> 00:12:08,539
And I timed them on my CPU, with four sequences, twelve heads, five hundred and twelve tokens: the manual version took a hundred and sixty six milliseconds and the fused one under fourteen.

144
00:12:08,539 --> 00:12:11,806
About twelve times faster, even without a GPU.

145
00:12:12,656 --> 00:12:13,549
Part seven.

146
00:12:13,549 --> 00:12:16,795
Autograd, nn dot Module, and embeddings.

147
00:12:17,645 --> 00:12:22,997
A gradient tells you how much the output changes when you nudge an input.

148
00:12:22,997 --> 00:12:27,910
Mark x as requires grad, and PyTorch records every operation on it.

149
00:12:27,910 --> 00:12:32,382
Here y is x squared and z is the sum of y, which is thirteen.

150
00:12:32,382 --> 00:12:38,541
Call z dot backward, and PyTorch walks the graph in reverse and fills in x dot grad.

151
00:12:38,541 --> 00:12:44,260
The derivative of x squared is two x, so at two and three we get four and six.

152
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:46,679
The notebook prints exactly that.

153
00:12:46,679 --> 00:12:55,697
To stop tracking, for example during inference, use torch dot no grad for a whole block, or dot detach for a single tensor.

154
00:12:56,547 --> 00:13:00,426
Every network in PyTorch inherits from nn dot Module.

155
00:13:00,426 --> 00:13:07,231
You define layers in init, after calling super init, and you define the data flow in forward.

156
00:13:07,231 --> 00:13:09,573
Calling model of x runs forward.

157
00:13:09,573 --> 00:13:17,622
The module automatically tracks every parameter, moves them all to a device at once, and saves and loads them.

158
00:13:17,622 --> 00:13:28,672
This is the book's example MLP, condensed onto one line in forward, with GPT-2's feed forward shapes: seven sixty eight to three oh seven two and back.

159
00:13:28,672 --> 00:13:36,648
I counted its parameters: four million seven hundred and twenty eight thousand, five hundred and seventy six.

160
00:13:37,498 --> 00:13:42,598
An embedding layer is just a lookup table: row i is the vector for token i.

161
00:13:42,598 --> 00:13:50,894
GPT-2 has fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven rows of seven sixty eight, thirty eight point six million parameters.

162
00:13:50,894 --> 00:13:57,014
Position embeddings are a second table, one thousand and twenty four by seven sixty eight.

163
00:13:57,014 --> 00:14:04,291
Position IDs are just arange of the sequence length, given a batch dimension and expanded across the batch.

164
00:14:04,291 --> 00:14:09,391
Add the two lookups, and that's what goes into the first transformer block.

165
00:14:09,391 --> 00:14:17,551
The notebook confirms the totals: thirty nine million, three hundred and eighty three thousand, eight hundred and eight.

166
00:14:17,551 --> 00:14:25,983
Token embeddings are about thirty one percent of GPT-2 small, which is why vocabulary size matters so much for small models.

167
00:14:26,833 --> 00:14:27,966
One correction.

168
00:14:27,966 --> 00:14:36,200
A gotcha box in the book says GPT-2 uses learned position embeddings, while BERT uses sinusoidal, fixed ones.

169
00:14:36,200 --> 00:14:38,013
The GPT-2 part is right.

170
00:14:38,013 --> 00:14:47,305
But BERT also uses learned absolute position embeddings, a five hundred and twelve by seven sixty eight table in BERT base.

171
00:14:47,305 --> 00:14:51,913
Sinusoidal encodings belong to the original 2017 transformer.

172
00:14:51,913 --> 00:14:53,499
That's in the errata.

173
00:14:54,349 --> 00:14:55,013
Part eight.

174
00:14:55,013 --> 00:14:56,099
The training loop.

175
00:14:56,949 --> 00:14:59,520
Training is always the same five steps.

176
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:04,992
One: zero the gradients, because PyTorch adds new gradients to old ones by default.

177
00:15:04,992 --> 00:15:07,761
Two: the forward pass, to get predictions.

178
00:15:07,761 --> 00:15:09,343
Three: compute the loss.

179
00:15:09,343 --> 00:15:13,298
Four: the backward pass, to fill every parameter's gradient.

180
00:15:13,298 --> 00:15:19,298
For LLMs, clip the gradients here, between backward and step, to prevent explosive updates.

181
00:15:19,298 --> 00:15:21,671
A max norm of one is a safe default.

182
00:15:21,671 --> 00:15:24,638
Five: the optimizer step updates the weights.

183
00:15:24,638 --> 00:15:25,429
Then repeat.

184
00:15:26,279 --> 00:15:29,078
Here's the notebook's loop, condensed.

185
00:15:29,078 --> 00:15:36,664
A small MLP, the Adam optimizer, cross entropy loss, and sixty four random examples with random labels.

186
00:15:36,664 --> 00:15:42,556
Each epoch: train mode, zero the gradients, forward, loss, backward, clip, step.

187
00:15:42,556 --> 00:15:46,459
Then evaluation mode and no grad to measure accuracy.

188
00:15:46,459 --> 00:15:55,887
My run printed losses of point six nine five, point six nine two and point six eight nine, and an accuracy of fifty six percent.

189
00:15:55,887 --> 00:16:01,558
Basically chance, because the labels are random and we only took three steps.

190
00:16:02,408 --> 00:16:08,638
I wondered what happens if you keep going, so I ran the same loop for three hundred steps.

191
00:16:08,638 --> 00:16:16,459
The loss falls from point six nine two to point zero five eight, and training accuracy reaches a hundred percent.

192
00:16:16,459 --> 00:16:17,636
On random labels!

193
00:16:17,636 --> 00:16:23,658
There's nothing to learn, so the model has simply memorised sixty four random examples.

194
00:16:23,658 --> 00:16:29,265
That's a powerful lesson for later: a falling training loss alone proves nothing.

195
00:16:29,265 --> 00:16:34,871
You always need a separate validation set, which we'll use when we train our LLM.

196
00:16:35,721 --> 00:16:38,232
A few switches that confuse everybody.

197
00:16:38,232 --> 00:16:44,443
model dot train turns dropout on, and model dot eval turns it off for deterministic behaviour.

198
00:16:44,443 --> 00:16:51,910
torch dot no grad stops building the computation graph for a whole block of code, saving memory during inference.

199
00:16:51,910 --> 00:16:55,148
dot detach cuts a single tensor out of the graph.

200
00:16:55,148 --> 00:17:04,927
And zero grad with set to none equals true sets the gradients to None instead of filling them with zeros, which saves memory and is slightly faster.

201
00:17:05,777 --> 00:17:06,536
Part nine.

202
00:17:06,536 --> 00:17:09,041
The exercises, with my solutions.

203
00:17:09,891 --> 00:17:13,677
Exercise two simulates splitting into attention heads.

204
00:17:13,677 --> 00:17:26,296
Take a two by five by five twelve tensor, reshape the last dimension into eight heads of sixty four, then permute so heads come before sequence: two by eight by five by sixty four.

205
00:17:26,296 --> 00:17:35,199
Now a batched matmul with its own transpose gives scores of two by eight by five by five, one score grid per head, as expected.

206
00:17:35,199 --> 00:17:40,527
And note that after permute the tensor isn't contiguous, so view would fail.

207
00:17:40,527 --> 00:17:48,519
This exact reshape and permute is how multi head attention splits the model width, which we'll see in chapter ten.

208
00:17:49,369 --> 00:17:53,010
Exercise three: one training step on a tiny network.

209
00:17:53,010 --> 00:18:02,112
My loss went from one point six seven seven to one point six oh eight after a single step, with a learning rate of point zero one.

210
00:18:02,112 --> 00:18:07,713
For the bonus question, how do you know whether gradients were actually clipped?

211
00:18:07,713 --> 00:18:12,123
clip grad norm returns the total gradient norm before clipping.

212
00:18:12,123 --> 00:18:19,124
Mine was point five five five, which is below the max norm of one, so nothing was clipped this time.

213
00:18:19,124 --> 00:18:21,785
Log that number in real training runs.

214
00:18:21,785 --> 00:18:25,075
It's one of the best early warnings of trouble.

215
00:18:25,925 --> 00:18:26,620
Part ten.

216
00:18:26,620 --> 00:18:27,547
Wrapping up.

217
00:18:28,397 --> 00:18:29,775
The key takeaways.

218
00:18:29,775 --> 00:18:34,600
One: tensors are arrays with shapes, so think how many of what.

219
00:18:34,600 --> 00:18:40,727
Two: PyTorch is NumPy plus GPUs plus autograd, with float thirty two by default.

220
00:18:40,727 --> 00:18:50,530
Three: the LLM shapes go from batch by sequence, to batch by sequence by model width, to batch by heads by sequence by sequence.

221
00:18:50,530 --> 00:18:58,035
Four: broadcasting aligns shapes from the right, expanding dimensions of size one or missing ones.

222
00:18:58,035 --> 00:19:05,847
Five: matrix multiplication combines features, and attention is four lines of code, or one fused call.

223
00:19:05,847 --> 00:19:11,591
Six: the training recipe is zero grad, forward, loss, backward, clip, step.

224
00:19:12,441 --> 00:19:14,709
My notebook run and book check.

225
00:19:14,709 --> 00:19:26,194
The chapter six notebook ran end to end on a CPU, all nineteen code cells, with no errors, and the shapes, parameter counts and gradients all match the book.

226
00:19:26,194 --> 00:19:33,729
My own checks of the default data types, zero copy sharing and fused attention all agree with the text.

227
00:19:33,729 --> 00:19:38,264
One erratum: BERT uses learned positions, not sinusoidal ones.

228
00:19:38,264 --> 00:19:44,336
And a note: the book's two to four times speed-up refers to FlashAttention on GPUs.

229
00:19:44,336 --> 00:19:53,992
On my CPU, the fused call was about twelve times faster than the manual version, mostly from avoiding all those separate operations.

230
00:19:54,842 --> 00:19:57,174
Review questions, with my answers.

231
00:19:57,174 --> 00:19:59,780
One: why the different default floats?

232
00:19:59,780 --> 00:20:03,552
NumPy targets scientific precision, so sixty four bits.

233
00:20:03,552 --> 00:20:07,186
PyTorch targets speed and memory, so thirty two bits.

234
00:20:07,186 --> 00:20:14,455
It matters when you mix them, which can cause dtype errors, and for calculations that need high precision.

235
00:20:14,455 --> 00:20:22,410
Two: view needs contiguous memory, while reshape copies when needed, so prefer reshape after a transpose or permute.

236
00:20:22,410 --> 00:20:31,737
Three: broadcasting aligns shapes from the right, and each pair must be equal or contain a one, with missing dimensions counting as one.

237
00:20:31,737 --> 00:20:37,498
Four: the dim in softmax is the axis that sums to one, usually minus one for logits.

238
00:20:37,498 --> 00:20:44,287
Pick the wrong one and you normalise across the batch or across positions, and it's silently wrong.

239
00:20:45,137 --> 00:20:53,962
Five: torch dot no grad disables gradient tracking for a whole block, while detach returns a single tensor with no history.

240
00:20:53,962 --> 00:20:56,401
Six: why zero the gradients first?

241
00:20:56,401 --> 00:21:04,222
Because gradients accumulate, so without clearing them each step would add the old gradients to the new ones.

242
00:21:04,222 --> 00:21:06,374
Set to none also saves memory.

243
00:21:06,374 --> 00:21:17,280
Seven: switching from train to eval turns off dropout and changes batch norm to use stored statistics, so evaluation and inference become deterministic.

244
00:21:18,130 --> 00:21:19,399
Your practice list.

245
00:21:19,399 --> 00:21:24,744
Run the chapter six notebook in Colab, and try a GPU runtime to compare timings.

246
00:21:24,744 --> 00:21:28,418
Print the shape after every line of the attention cell.

247
00:21:28,418 --> 00:21:32,427
Break broadcasting on purpose, and read the error carefully.

248
00:21:32,427 --> 00:21:37,371
Run the training loop for three hundred steps and watch it memorise noise.

249
00:21:37,371 --> 00:21:40,978
And solve the three exercises plus the clipping bonus.

250
00:21:41,828 --> 00:21:43,243
That's chapter six.

251
00:21:43,243 --> 00:21:48,904
You can now read and write the tensor code at the heart of every modern LLM.

252
